<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menkaure</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">26th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mycerinus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menkaure was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Fourth Dynasty in the 26th century BCE, probably a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>. He built the third and smallest of the main pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. Statues from his valley temple, including the greywacke triads and the pair statue with his queen, are among the finest works of Old Kingdom sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menkaure.jpg" alt="Menkaure" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iry-Hor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menkaura_Bust_Closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menkaure is known in Greek as Mykerinos, Latinized Mycerinus, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> as Menkheres. Manetho names Bikheris as his predecessor, but the archaeological evidence points almost certainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>. A flint knife from his mortuary temple mentions a king's mother, Khamerernebty I, which suggests that she and Khafre were his parents. His brothers Nebemakhet, Duaenre, Nikaure and Iunmin served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> during his reign. Queen Khamerernebty II was the mother of a king's son, Khuenre, who probably died before his father, and the throne passed to Shepseskaf, a younger son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The length of the reign is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives 63 years, which is surely an exaggeration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> is damaged where the total should stand, and Egyptologists generally accept that 18 years was meant. A workmen's graffito mentions the year after the 11th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> count; if the count was held every second year, he might have ruled for 22 years. In 2013 a fragment of a sphinx of Menkaure was found at Tel Hazor.</p><p>His pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, called Netjer-er-Menkaure, meaning Menkaure is Divine, measures 103.4 metres at the base and 65.5 metres in height, the smallest of the three main pyramids there. Three subsidiary pyramids stand beside it. His valley temple, mainly of brick, yielded the statues for which he is best known: greywacke triads showing the king with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> and the deities of different <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a>, and a double statue of the king with a queen, probably Khamerernebty II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1837 Richard William Howard Vyse and John Shae Perring entered the pyramid and found a basalt sarcophagus in the burial chamber, undecorated by hieroglyphs, beside wooden coffin fragments bearing the king's name and a partial skeleton. The ship carrying the sarcophagus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was lost after leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in October 1838. The coffin is now thought to be a replacement made during the Saite period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> placed the bones in the Coptic period.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, writing long afterwards, made Menkaure a pious son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> whose only daughter died before him and whom an oracle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a> doomed to a short life. He is said to have turned night into day by revelling, to double his remaining six years. This is a late tradition, not contemporary evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Menkaure” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fourth Dynasty (2613–2494 BCE) was the zenith of royal power: Sneferu, then his son Khufu (2589–2566 BCE), whose sons Djedefre and Khafre may have quarrelled, then <b>Menkaure</b>, Shepseskaf and perhaps Djedefptah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Some new spells claimed an older origin: the rubric to Spell 30B says that Prince Hordjedef found it in the reign of King <b>Menkaure</b>, centuries before it is attested.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
